      "reasoning": "The selected passage and inherited context match the subject of the translation. Inspection used the supplied renderings; hashes were not independently recomputed."
    },
    {
      "id": "C02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 1 describes scalar contaminant density q(x,t) and constant real velocity ū.",
      "translation_evidence": "The fields take values in Fin 1→Real, canonically identifiable with Real; position, time, and fixed speed are real.",
      "reasoning": "The singleton coordinate representation preserves the scalar state and constant velocity."
    },
    {
      "id": "C03",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) concerns any two spatial endpoints. The recorded interpretation specifies an almost-everywhere mass-rate identity for each fixed spatial interval.",
      "translation_evidence": "Balance holds for every a,b,s,t; the derivative assertion has order ∀a,b, almost every t.",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded interpretation, interval dependence of the exceptional null set is preserved. The nonzero rectangle witness may depend on the fields."
    },
    {
      "id": "C04",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The book leaves analytic conditions qualitative. The recorded interpretation selects signed locally integrable production densities and finite interval mass/face-flux integrals.",
      "translation_evidence": "Production has integrable spatial slices at every time and locally integrable signed spatial integrals as functions of time; joint measurability and joint absolute integrability are not required explicitly.",
      "reasoning": "It is unresolved whether the selected local integrability convention means joint space-time local integrability or precisely these iterated conditions. They are not interchangeable without additional justification."
    },
    {
      "id": "C05",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The passage distinguishes endpoint transport from nonconservation requiring source terms. The recorded interpretation adds rectangle balance and a fixed-interval almost-everywhere rate identity.",
      "translation_evidence": "The conclusions identify integrated production with mass change minus boundary transport, characterize homogeneous balance by zero production integrals, and give the almost-everywhere rate identity.",
      "reasoning": "These conclusions cover the selected interpreted claim. They do not establish the sourced model from independent physical assumptions; rectangle balance is the assumed governing model."
    },
    {
      "id": "C06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Equation (1.10) uses interval mass and left-endpoint flux minus right-endpoint flux.",
      "translation_evidence": "The supplied ledger explains both local balance predicates, oriented Bochner integration, Lebesgue almost-everywhere quantification, genuine derivative existence, and pointwise scalar operations.",
      "reasoning": "The supplied dependency meanings consistently explain the translated operators. This assessment uses the permitted translation ledger, not independent inspection of Lean declarations."
    },
    {
      "id": "C07",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The printed conditional asserts necessity of source terms without asserting a converse or a nonzero criterion.",
      "translation_evidence": "Under assumed rectangle balance, homogeneous balance is equivalent to all integrated production terms vanishing, and its failure is equivalent to a nonzero rectangle integral.",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded interpretation, these equivalences follow by subtracting the common boundary contribution. They characterize failure of homogeneous balance, not mere presence of a syntactic source term, and are not attributed to the printed passage alone."
    },
    {
      "id": "C08",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "Printed page 4 allows interval mass to change through endpoint flux while distinguishing internal contaminant nonconservation.",
      "translation_evidence": "Q is interval content, B is boundary flux difference, and P is integrated production; P equals content change minus integrated B.",
      "reasoning": "The translation preserves the distinction between transport and internal production."
    },
    {
      "id": "C09",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The flux is f(q)=ūq, with endpoint signs f(q(x₁,t))−f(q(x₂,t)).",
      "translation_evidence": "Flux is c·q and B_ab=c·q(a,t)−c·q(b,t), with a fixed scalar c.",
      "reasoning": "Coefficients, signs, and parameter dependence agree. The positive additive production convention is supplied by the interpreted balance, not explicitly by the book."
    },
    {
      "id": "C10",
      "status": "unclear",
      "source_evidence": "The recorded interpretation excludes general singular production measures and selects locally integrable densities.",
      "translation_evidence": "Production is a function subject to slice and iterated-integral conditions, without explicit joint local integrability.",
      "reasoning": "Exclusion of measure-valued production is preserved, but the exact function class may extend beyond or differ from the selected density class. Resolving this is necessary to assess applicability."
    },
    {
      "id": "C11",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source imposes no endpoint ordering and retains integral conservation at discontinuities.",
      "translation_evidence": "Integrals are oriented; equal endpoints give zero; speed may vanish; only the interval mass derivative is asserted almost everywhere.",
      "reasoning": "Reversed and degenerate intervals are coherent, and no classical pointwise PDE is imposed at discontinuities."
    },
    {
      "id": "C12",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The selected passage includes both conserved transport and internal mass change.",
      "translation_evidence": "The assumptions allow zero fields and also q(x,t)=t, production(x,t)=1 at the sole coordinate, with arbitrary fixed speed.",
      "reasoning": "For the latter example boundary flux cancels, interval mass change equals integrated production, and nondegenerate rectangles can have nonzero production. The substantive conclusions are not restricted to zero production."
    },
    {
      "id": "N01",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The physical process is contaminant transport at constant velocity, with possible internal mass production or destruction.",
      "translation_evidence": "The flux remains c·q and the separate production contribution enters interval balance.",
      "reasoning": "The interpreted statement remains linked to that transport process. No numerical algorithm is claimed."
    },
    {
      "id": "N02",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The contaminant example is scalar.",
      "translation_evidence": "The singleton product has componentwise operations and the absolute-value norm on its sole real coordinate.",
      "reasoning": "The analytic infrastructure preserves scalar semantics; no alternative norm estimate is introduced."
    },
    {
      "id": "N03",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected passage concerns a physical conservation balance, not an error or stability bound.",
      "translation_evidence": "No approximation error, conditioning, or stability quantity appears.",
      "reasoning": "The balance defect is interpreted as production, with no substitution among numerical error notions."
    },
    {
      "id": "N04",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source uses exact density, flux, and interval mass.",
      "translation_evidence": "All fields and integrals are exact mathematical quantities.",
      "reasoning": "No computed cell average or approximate numerical flux replaces the source objects."
    },
    {
      "id": "N05",
      "status": "not-applicable",
      "source_evidence": "The selected contaminant passage has no asymptotic expansion or truncation claim.",
      "translation_evidence": "The conclusions are exact identities and logical equivalences.",
      "reasoning": "There are no higher-order terms or asymptotic regimes to compare."
    },
    {
      "id": "N06",
      "status": "pass",
      "source_evidence": "The source qualifies classical differential conversion by smoothness; the recorded interpretation instead retains an almost-everywhere interval mass-rate identity.",
      "translation_evidence": "For each fixed interval, integrable boundary and production terms determine mass increments, and the conclusion asserts genuine derivative existence almost everywhere.",
      "reasoning": "The rate conclusion follows from the indefinite-integral representation in the translated setting. It neither asserts pointwise differentiability of q nor claims existence or uniqueness of solutions."
    }
  ],
  "implications": {
    "translation_implies_source": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation, the translation captures the intended distinction between transport and production and the necessity of nonzero integrated production when homogeneous balance fails. However, complete implication for the exact selected domain remains unresolved because 'locally integrable production densities' is not identified explicitly with the translation's slice and iterated-integral conditions. The printed source alone does not resolve this analytic convention."
    },
    "source_implies_translation": {
      "verdict": "unclear",
      "reasoning": "Under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation, the algebraic conclusions follow from rectangle balance and the derivative conclusion follows from the translated integrability assumptions. What remains unestablished is whether the convention supplies precisely those assumptions at every spatial and temporal slice. Joint local integrability ordinarily controls slices only almost everywhere and does not by itself specify the representatives needed for every-endpoint balance. Thus full implication cannot be certified from the supplied evidence."
    }
  },
  "classification": "undetermined",
  "accepted": false,
  "requires_adjudication": true,
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "category": "production-domain-and-slice-regularity",
      "source_evidence": "The book does not specify a production function class. The separate recorded interpretation selects signed locally integrable production densities, finite interval mass/face-flux integrals, rectangle balance, and fixed-interval almost-everywhere mass rates.",
      "translation_evidence": "The hypothesis requires spatial production integrability at every time and temporal integrability of each signed spatial production integral. It does not explicitly require joint measurability or absolute integrability on space-time rectangles.",
      "impact": "These analytic conditions do not automatically identify the same domain as joint local integrability. Adjudication must establish the intended meaning of the recorded convention and whether every-slice requirements and representatives are included. Both full implication directions remain unresolved."
    },
    {
      "severity": "note",
      "category": "interpretation-qualified-conclusions",
      "source_evidence": "The selected printed passage supplies a physical conditional, with chemical reactions as an example, and defers source-term mathematics.",
      "translation_evidence": "The theorem assumes a sourced rectangle balance and derives integrated-production characterizations and an almost-everywhere rate formula.",
      "impact": "These are appropriate mathematical consequences of the interpreted governing model, not explicit claims or analytic hypotheses printed in the selected passage. Any later acceptance must retain that qualification."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The attached source renderings and supplied translation agree on scalar constant-speed transport, interval mass, boundary-flux signs, and the distinction between transport and internal production. Under the recorded coordinator-selected interpretation, the additional algebraic characterizations and almost-everywhere rate conclusion are coherent and nonvacuous. The unresolved issue is the exact analytic domain: the selected locally integrable density convention is not explicitly equated with the translated every-slice and iterated-integral conditions. Because that affects applicability and both implication directions, adjudication is required. No filesystem, network, Lean, or prior audit evidence was accessed."
